I’ll first inventory the measurement, metadata, and reusable refinement utilities, then establish the chemically constrained candidate set before fitting. I’ll keep source competitors separate, health-screen every candidate, and only retain phases that survive replay-stable GSAS-II refinement and residual-family checks.

The chemistry is a constrained Pb–W–O system made from WO₃ and Pb₃O₄ at 500 °C. Because no precursor amounts are reported, absolute Pb:W closure cannot be fixed numerically; I’ll document that limitation and test the charge-balanced likely products (including non-1:1 lead tungstates and residual oxides) against the full pattern rather than assume PbWO₄ alone.

I found exact COD competitors for scheelite and raspite PbWO₄, multiple WO₃ polymorphs, Pb₃O₄, PbO, and PbO₂. I’m now health-screening those records before any refinement and will use whole-pattern matching to distinguish genuine coexisting phases from alternate records of the same phase.

Peak-family matching already shows two coherent inventories: scheelite PbWO₄ accounts for the 27.81/45.13/55.63/72.36° families, while monoclinic WO₃ accounts independently for the 23.53/24.02/24.84° triplet and its 33–34°, 42°, and 50° families. Both sets share an approximately +0.4° angular offset, so I’ll explicitly compare a single zero correction with Bragg–Brentano sample displacement before refining cells.

The neutral baseline confirms both phases with positive scales. Once cells and the common nonnegative U/W/X profile are refined, PbWO₄ requires real phase-specific broadening (about 0.06 µm coherent size), whereas WO₃ remains effectively instrument-limited at the neutral 10 µm value. The current two-phase fit is ~74.2 wt% PbWO₄ / 25.8 wt% WO₃, but \(R_{wp}\approx8.8\%\) remains well above the counting-statistical expectation, so I’m not treating that as final; I’m now interrogating residual families, source alternatives, angular/profile causes, and intensity corrections.

The residuals contain a third, independent fingerprint rather than a profile artifact: 26.81°, 31.22°, 32.55°, 34.47°, 50.29°, 52.52°, and 58.48° align with tetragonal minium Pb₃O₄ after the same angular correction. That is chemically credible as unreacted precursor and satisfies the “two non-coincident families” gate many times over. I’m adding the passing Pb₃O₄ record first with a neutral 10 µm/zero-strain model and scale-only test, then I’ll reconverge the full nuisance model.

A second residual fingerprint remains after minium: the isolated 29.56° maximum, plus 30.89°, 38.25°, 45.7°, 53.6°, and ~60.9° match orthorhombic massicot PbO under the same correction. This is also charge/thermal-context plausible from Pb₃O₄ at 500 °C. I’m applying the same neutral scale-first test to the passing massicot record; its tetragonal litharge and alternate Pbcm records remain competitors, not extra phases.

The four-phase inventory now has positive, replay-stable scales, but the isolated Pb₃O₄ 26.81° line is much broader than the common profile (observed FWHM ≈0.46°, calculated ≈0.19°), while the sharp WO₃ triplet is slightly overbroadened. I’m therefore comparing Pb₃O₄ crystallite-size and microstrain models from the identical saved baseline; this is the targeted profile-cause test required before considering any further phase.

The matched test favors finite Pb₃O₄ size over strain (\(R_{wp}=4.41\%\) vs 4.50%; ~37 nm size versus an implausibly large ~13,000 ppm strain). I’m retaining the simpler size model. This also sharpens the common profile and raises the physically integrated Pb₃O₄ fraction, as expected for a broadened phase. Next I’m checking whether the weak PbO peaks need their own broadening and whether any coherent unmodeled families remain.

Background-order replay supports six Chebyshev terms: four and five terms materially worsen \(R_{wp}\) (5.07% and 4.26%), while eight terms only reach 4.13% by adding highly correlated, oscillatory coefficients. I’m retaining six terms and will now test polarization and surface roughness before any texture model, as required.

Surface roughness is the physically admissible angular-intensity correction: refining Suortti A/B improves \(R_{wp}\) to about 3.25% with interior, significant values, whereas polarization alone tried to exceed its physical limit and is rejected. I’m checking whether polarization remains identifiable once roughness is present; if it stays bound or correlated, it will be fixed and pruned.

The combined polarization/roughness replay is slow and strongly coupled, which itself is a warning against retaining both. I’m letting the saved refinement finish once so I can judge the covariance and then will revert to the simpler physical model if polarization is non-identifiable.

The physically stable baseline is now \(R_{wp}=3.32\%\) versus \(R_{exp}=2.49\%\), with a single positive roughness parameter and no bound nuisance term. Before freezing it, I’m testing only low-order March–Dollase texture: symmetry-distinct axial [001] and diagonal [110] models for the dominant scheelite phase, then I’ll keep a term only if it is replay-stable and complementary.

The low-order texture challenge is negative: both [001] and [110] scheelite March ratios refine back to ~1.00 with negligible fit gain, so texture is rejected. The alternate angular model also needs an implausible ~−0.73 mm sample displacement for essentially the same fit, so the single refined zero correction is retained. I’m now doing one-at-a-time source substitutions so alternate records/polymorphs cannot masquerade as simultaneous phases.

The stable four-phase model is now clear: scheelite PbWO₄, monoclinic WO₃, minium Pb₃O₄, and massicot PbO. I’m finishing the required alternate-structure challenges and watch-list tests, then I’ll replay the selected model from disk and freeze all requested outputs from that reopened project.

The first matched-nuisance comparison batch is running against the same saved baseline. It includes a second scheelite record, raspite PbWO₄, an alternate monoclinic WO₃ record, and orthorhombic Pb₃O₄; each candidate is being tested scale-first before allowing its cell or phase broadening to move.

These refinements are CPU-bound and still progressing without errors. While they converge, the key discriminant remains whether an alternate reproduces the full peak-family pattern under the identical background, instrument, roughness, and broadening treatment—not simply whether it can absorb one strong line.

The first results strongly favor the retained records. Raspite’s scale collapsed to zero; alternate WO₃ and orthorhombic Pb₃O₄ models leave much larger residuals; even the second scheelite record is materially poorer after matched treatment. I’m now checking the PbO polymorph/source alternatives and the reviewed minium record, whose short O–O contact must be weighed against a healthy equivalent model rather than rejected by label alone.

The reviewed minium entry does fit the same phase family, but it is still worse than the healthy retained entry (Rwp 3.48% versus 3.32%) and contains an implausibly short O–O contact, so there is no chemical reason to retain it. A triclinic WO₃ competitor is closer (3.41%) but remains inferior and less stable; the alternate massicot record is poorer and drives its cell nonphysically. I’m now re-challenging the converged inventory by adding plausible polymorph/redox candidates as genuinely neutral, scale-only watch phases.

The watch-list replay behaves as required: raspite and Pb₇W₈O₂₈.₈ collapse to zero scale. Litharge and PbO₂ settle at tiny positive scales but change Rwp by less than 0.001 percentage point; I’ll verify their implied fractions and residual families, but that is not enough evidence to add either phase. The retained four-phase inventory will now be replayed as the final project with only supported terms active.

The full-range disk replay converged twice with maximum shift/esd 0.004, and the reopened project gives Rwp 3.3193%, GOF 1.338. The normalized crystalline fractions are 58.284% PbWO₄, 19.820% WO₃, 17.034% Pb₃O₄, and 4.862% PbO. I’m now health-screening the exported CIFs and independently validating the JSON/project/profile agreement and geometry.

All four final CIFs pass the occupancy/contact health screen. The two tiny watch scales correspond to only 0.050(25) wt% litharge and 0.028(27) wt% PbO₂; their strongest non-overlapped diagnostic lines have absent or negative residuals, while the small positive residuals coincide with retained-phase families. They therefore fail the two-independent-family gate and are excluded.

The chemistry is also internally consistent, with one important limit: the metadata gives no precursor amounts, so no fixed feed ratio exists to enforce. The crystalline result implies Pb:W = 1.0507 and an equivalent Pb₃O₄:WO₃ ratio of 0.3502 (near the 1:3 reaction ratio), with residual precursors and PbO explained by incomplete conversion/redox. I’ve documented that as a consistency check—not as measured feed closure—and noted that amorphous content cannot be quantified.

Completed the four-phase GSAS-II refinement.

| Crystalline phase | Weight fraction |
|---|---:|
